WEBVTT

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.280
Winter in Antarctica.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:16.480
The temperature has dropped to minus 70 degrees centigrade and winds of 120 miles an hour

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.480
blow across the desolate ice scape.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.240
The centre of Antarctica is in continuous darkness.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.400
Only its fringes see the bleak winter light.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:36.760
The sea freezes over for hundreds of miles, effectively doubling the size of the continent.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.080
In winter, the Antarctic is a very lonely place.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:46.220
As the temperature plummets and the sea ice forms, most of the wildlife that came down

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.440
here to take advantage of the brief summer season is forced to retreat north again.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:58.960
Practically nothing stays, for to survive in the deep south at its most bitterly hostile

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.000
requires a very special animal with very special adaptations.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.420
And just such a creature is the Weddell Seal.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:15.400
No other mammal lives throughout the year as far south as this.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:22.060
These seals are just 800 miles from the pole and they stay here winter and summer.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.920
Like all Antarctic seals, they have a thick layer of blubber to insulate them from the

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.800
cold, but the real key to their success in surviving here is their ability to keep open

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:38.560
holes in the ice so that they have access to the sea the year round.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.880
The holes made by the seals are the only things that break the white monotony over hundreds

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.520
of square miles of sea ice.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:52.640
The seals, with no escape to the open ocean, are forced to stay near the holes.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:03:00.480
Such is a gateway to and from the underwater world in which the seals hunt and find shelter.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.560
Underwater, the temperature never drops below minus 1.8 degrees.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:23.880
The seals retreat down here during the worst winter storms and so keep comparatively warm.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:31.160
When you dive beneath the ice, you enter, within seconds, a totally different world.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.680
Here within a foot or so of the gale-swept, savagely cold wilderness above, illuminated

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:44.080
only by the dim blue light filtering through the ceiling of ice, there is stability, peace

00:03:44.080 --> 00:04:10.880
and an eerie, unforgettable beauty.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:32.560
Animals need special adaptations to live in water that is below 0 centigrade.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.160
Most fish would explode if they touched this glacier wall.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.080
Crystals would immediately form in their cells.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:50.520
These survive because their tissues are loaded with antifreeze.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.880
Life beneath the ice, compared with the white desert above, is extraordinarily rich.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:19.320
There are all kinds of invertebrates, including giant jellyfish.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:34.600
It's a very sheltered place, for the permanent sea ice overhead provides year-round protection

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.560
from waves and storms.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:43.280
But food is scarce and many of these creatures have become scavengers.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:51.280
These starfish make a meal of seal feces.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.080
Small seals can dive to 750 metres, possibly more, in search of food.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:07.520
At these depths in permanent darkness, they encounter a world dominated by stalked sponges.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:22.320
Growing extremely slowly in the cold, the Antarctic invertebrates become giants.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.840
Returning from depths where a human being would be crushed, seals surface, suffering

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:40.720
none of the effects of deep diving that can cripple human swimmers.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.880
October in the far south.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:56.960
Female weddle seals haul out on the sea ice to give birth.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.880
Imagine the shock of leaving a womb at plus 37 degrees centigrade and being dropped on

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:30.280
the ice into a world at minus 20.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:40.440
The pup has to suckle and build a layer of blubber as fast as possible.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:46.640
It usually manages to double its weight in 10 days, for weddle milk is 60% fat, one of

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.520
the richest produced by any mammal.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:57.960
Remarkably, after one week, the pup is ready for a swim.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:16.360
The mother is anxious to get her pup accustomed to the water before the weather deteriorates.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:33.600
At this time more than any other, breathing holes are jealously guarded.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:09:03.400
The

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:09.400
weddles have an especially wide gait and long canine and incisor teeth which enable them

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.480
to scrape away the ice that is constantly forming and threatening to close their breathing

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.440
holes.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.000
Their teeth aren't impervious to this wear and tear and are gradually worn down so that

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.280
eventually the seal can't eat.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:32.880
As a consequence, weddles die at about 20 years, half the age of other Antarctic seals.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.800
A male defends an underwater territory and mates with all the females that use his breathing

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:38.800
holes.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.640
It's an effective way of acquiring a harem because females must have a refuge below the

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:51.600
ice from the extremes of the winter weather.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.560
It might seem that there could not be a more harsh existence than this, but the environment

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:02.480
here is comparatively constant and these seals are adapted to it, protected by a coat of

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.320
dense hair and insulated by blubber immediately beneath the skin.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.720
Indeed, weddles do far better than most other seals.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:19.240
If they are sufficiently fattened in the six weeks before their wean, 95% of pups will

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:27.480
survive.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:33.360
These seals, the most southerly in the world, live in the shadow of the largest active volcano

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:41.560
in Antarctica, Mount Erebus.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.600
Erebus is a mountain of extremes.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:51.160
In the crater, molten lava bubbles away at 600 degrees centigrade and yet on the summit,

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.680
temperatures rarely rise above minus 45 degrees.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:57.800
And here there is life.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:03.440
The heat of the volcano produces steam that rises to the rim and melts the snow and ice,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.560
leaving bare patches of rock, home to heat-loving bacteria and algae.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.800
Another extraordinary example of how life can survive in the most extreme conditions

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:31.640
on Earth.

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:36.840
Behind Mount Erebus, the trans-Antarctic mountains stretch in a long broadband.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:43.280
They're the most extensive range on the continent, running for some 2,000 miles and separating

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:51.160
the great east and west ice caps.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:56.320
Although many of the peaks are over 4,000 meters high, most of the range is blanketed

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:17.680
by vast glaciers, which fill the valleys.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:23.160
Hidden among the trans-Antarctic mountains is one of the continent's greatest surprises,

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:25.120
the dry valleys.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:31.600
China is the largest area of bare rock to be found in Antarctica.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:38.080
It's so arid that falling snow soon evaporates and never builds up.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:40.760
The valley below me is the driest place on Earth.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.800
It hasn't snowed or rained here for centuries.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:49.200
In winter, the temperature falls to minus 52 degrees centigrade and the ground is permanently

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.720
frozen to a depth of almost half a mile.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:57.680
In fact, conditions are so extreme that when scientists came to design a vehicle to work

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:02.520
on the surface of Mars, they brought it to this valley in order to test it.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:07.600
And a clue as to the factor that creates these conditions lies in the extraordinary

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.000
shape of these boulders.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:16.880
Although they are solid granite, they've been carved by savage winds that scream down off

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.600
the ice cap.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:24.720
These winds are so dry that they instantly absorb any moisture in the air and by doing

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:29.120
so they will desiccate and preserve organic tissues.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:35.120
This mummified crab eater seal, about 70 miles from the sea, has probably been lying here

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:40.160
for 3,000 years or more.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:46.820
You might suppose that a place that can freeze dry the body of seals for centuries would

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:50.120
be totally without life.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:55.840
But even in these extreme conditions, life does exist.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.840
Pick the right sort of rock.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.980
This is a light porous sandstone.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:09.960
Give it a hit and there, a millimetre beneath the surface, within the actual fabric of the

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:15.760
rock, a band of green, the colour of life.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:20.720
It's a lichen that has managed to penetrate and colonise the microscopic spaces between

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.160
the grains of the porous rock.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:28.760
It's the only place where it can survive in these arid desert-like conditions.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:38.720
Above the dry valleys, held back by the trans-Antarctic mountains, stretches the ice cap itself.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:51.960
This is the Antarctic Plateau, 3,000 metres high.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:59.760
There can be no more forbidding hostile desolate places to be than up here on the Antarctic

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.480
Plateau.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:04.960
It's not just that human life here seems insignificant.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:09.520
It seems totally irrelevant.

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:18.080
A few spots of lichens may grow on boulders to within 200 miles of the South Pole and

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:24.680
in the summer, maybe one or two particularly adventurous snow petals will come up here

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.400
to try a nest.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:40.760
And come the winter, absolutely nothing living moves up here on the Antarctic Plateau.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:46.560
Even in summer, it's always winter here, with temperatures averaging minus 30.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.040
One and a half times the size of Australia, this is the largest area of lifeless wilderness

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.960
in the world.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:59.880
Two petrels, brief visitors here in summer, are forced to spend the winter hundreds of

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:10.860
miles to the warmer north at the edge of the frozen sea.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:16.520
This is the frontier between life in the ocean and a desert of ice where almost no animals

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:17.960
dare go.

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:34.520
But one creature has to cross it, the emperor penguin.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:39.460
In May, when the freezing waters and the cold winter temperatures force other animals to

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:57.760
retreat to warmer conditions in the north, emperor penguins head south.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.940
They make their way to a number of traditional nesting sites.

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:10.460
In this one alone, there may be 25,000 birds.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:11.840
Emperors are unique.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:28.440
They are the only birds to lay their eggs directly on ice.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:33.920
But hours after the female has produced her single egg, the male takes it over.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:41.000
The transfer has to be quick if the egg is not to freeze.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:47.360
The male maneuvers it into a brood pouch lined with blood vessels that keep the egg 80 degrees

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:49.720
warmer than the outside temperature.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:18:00.360
There, under a flap of skin, it's sealed away for the winter.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:05.440
When the egg is safely inside the male's pouch, the females are free to go and they start

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:10.820
the long trek back across the sea ice to the open ocean, leaving their partners to face

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:31.320
the coldest conditions on earth.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:37.180
With temperatures of 70 below and in terrible storms, the penguins huddle tightly together

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:38.680
for warmth.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:43.760
No other adult penguins are so tolerant of one another, but for emperors, this is the

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:51.680
key to survival.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.920
The cooperation is not random.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:59.600
Those most exposed on the windward side move down around the huddle to the more sheltered

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:00.980
side.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:06.320
This ensures that every bird gets its fair share of warmth in the middle and, in time,

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:21.800
takes its turn in enduring the brunt of the Antarctic weather.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:27.600
As midwinter approaches, the sun disappears below the horizon for the last time this season.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:40.240
A month of total darkness lies ahead.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:45.840
Above the huddle, the southern lights, the aurora australis, blaze across the winter

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.240
sky.

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:55.160
These spectacular displays occur as subatomic particles travelling through space enter the

00:19:55.160 --> 00:20:18.960
earth's magnetic field.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:24.080
As winter recedes, the huddles begin to break up, and heat that was trapped within them

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.120
for so long escapes.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:48.480
These males, who have not eaten for 115 days, are close to death by starvation.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:54.240
As the sun returns to the southern hemisphere, the female emperors, sleek and fat from months

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:58.960
of feeding at sea, begin the long march back to the rookery.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:03.800
The sea ice is now at its fullest extent, and they may have to walk a hundred miles

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:15.800
to reach their colony.

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:23.560
By now, the eggs have hatched, and the tiny chicks are awaiting their first feed.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.960
Each female times her return to coincide with the hatching of her chick.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:34.000
A male, having starved for so long, can give the chick only one meal, no more than a milky

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.400
secretion from his gut wall.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:40.860
If his partner doesn't return within ten days of the chick's hatching, he will be forced

00:21:40.860 --> 00:22:00.200
to abandon it and head for the sea to find food for himself.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:02.400
It's a noisy time in the colony.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:07.600
The courtship calling that took place before winter now brings its reward.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:31.680
After a separation of over three months, a bird can still recognise its partner's call.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:36.520
When they find one another, the pair perform their greeting ritual, so making sure that

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.720
there hasn't been a case of mistaken identity.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:55.280
And then the female gives their chick its first proper meal, half-digested fish.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:00.360
She's very eager to take charge of the chick, but the male, having cared for it for so long,

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:02.320
is reluctant to give it up.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:15.680
She has literally to push him back to get him to release it.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:19.400
The transfer is a tricky manoeuvre that must be done fast.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:48.240
A chick left on the ice for only two minutes will die.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:54.160
The males, after their four-month ordeal, near to starvation and desperate to feed,

00:23:54.160 --> 00:24:01.920
have to walk a hundred miles or so back to the open sea.

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:06.680
Mothers and chicks spend the next few weeks learning each other's call and establishing

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:11.520
a strong bond that ensures that they will recognise one another in the months ahead

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:24.680
when she returns from feeding trips.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:37.760
It's early spring and the weather is still variable.

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:41.680
Severe storms are a real threat to the chicks' survival.

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:45.440
One that has been abandoned seeks shelter from passing adults.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:50.400
One of them seems interested, but the vital bond between parent and chick simply isn't

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:59.080
there and eventually the adult walks off.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:04.400
In fact, the adults do have a strong instinct to protect chicks, so much so that birds that

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:09.800
have not managed to breed will try to take possession of a stray or abandoned chick.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:14.880
But this fostering never succeeds because the adult has no partner to help in rearing

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:15.880
the waif.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:45.120
These desperate, unpartnered penguins will

00:25:45.120 --> 00:26:04.520
sometimes fight over a chick and crush it to death.

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:07.000
Mortality is high.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:13.640
Many eggs don't hatch and of those that do, 25% die in the first few months.

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:18.680
Chickens that survive have to grow fast and fledge before the sea ice on which they live

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:42.660
breaks up beneath them.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:46.080
These chicks take five months to rear.

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:50.180
Only by incubating the eggs through the harsh Antarctic winter so that the chicks hatch

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:55.200
at the very beginning of the short summer is it possible for the emperors to breed every

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:58.120
year.

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:04.240
It was to collect an emperor penguin's egg like this that men made the first ever land

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:09.000
journey in the bitter cold darkness of the Antarctic winter.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:14.200
Bill Wilson, the naturalist on Captain Scott's expedition, was fascinated about the evolutionary

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:20.240
origin of birds and he was convinced that the embryo in an egg like this would provide

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:26.440
conclusive evidence of the link between the feathers of birds and the scales of reptiles.

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:33.360
So on June 12th, 1911, in the middle of winter, he and two companions left Captain Scott's

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:40.520
hut here on Cape Evans and set out for the emperor penguin colony on the other side of

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:43.880
Mount Erebus 65 miles away.

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:51.560
It was a trip that became known with some justice as the worst journey in the world.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:53.280
The weather was abominable.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:57.980
Their clothes and harnesses froze solid and all three suffered terrible frostbite as they

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:01.840
hauled their sledges over heavily crevasse terrain.

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:06.040
On the return journey, they lost their tent in a violent storm.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:11.520
By a miracle, they found it again and then made it back to the hut alive.

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:16.640
They brought back three eggs and three emperor penguin skins, one of which is still here

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:21.240
in Scott's hut, preserved by the Antarctic cold.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:26.120
Although the connection between birds and reptiles is no longer in doubt, the eggs didn't

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.800
provide the evidence that Wilson thought they would.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:34.520
But even so, the journey remains one of the great epic stories in the annals of polar

00:28:34.520 --> 00:29:03.640
exploration.
